From 5ddfd4e342af974c2935aed9a06451345fac3593 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: DSH <dsh@local>
Date: Sun, 1 Nov 2026 14:16:36 +0800
Subject: [PATCH 2/2] feat(tts): queue native TTS across paragraph boundaries
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

A paragraph used to end with a stop, a flush and an engine restart: the
controller's handover called stop() on the engine, slept the inter-paragraph
gap, then started the next block cold. That restart — not the sentence
cadence — is the long pause heard between paragraphs.

Keep the queue instead. A block's sentences are submitted as one batch, the
controller's preloads append the following paragraphs behind them while the
current one is still speaking, and a handover no longer stops the engine, so
the next block starts from audio that is already queued. The block's marks are
matched against the queue front: a preloaded block costs no submission, while
anything else (a seek, a skip, a voice change) drops the stale queue and starts
clean. A block cut short by an abort marks the queue stale, so a seek can no
longer leave the old position playing behind the new one.

Capabilities gain queuedBlocks, which tells the controller not to sleep between
blocks: the engine already has the pause covered, and a sleep here would only
delay the preloads that keep the queue fed.
